    "title": "What to Know About the ‘Laguna Beach’ Reunion",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTThe Cast of ‘Laguna Beach’ Reunites Onscreen After 20 YearsThe show, which depicted the lives of a group of wealthy high schoolers and helped to pioneer a genre of soap-style reality TV, will return in April with a reunion special.Years after they finished high school, the stars of “Laguna Beach” are back in front of the cameras.Credit...Roku ChannelMarch 25, 2026Few reality TV shows captured a certain brand of mid-aughts teendom quite like “Laguna Beach,” the millennial favorite that debuted on MTV more than 20 years ago.It helped to pioneer the soap-style reality drama, and invited viewers to follow the lives of affluent seniors at Laguna Beach High School in Orange County, Calif. The show was rife with love triangles and Olympic-level mean-girling, and it was shot-through with the yearning and anticipatory nostalgia that the last year of high school often inspires.The draw of “Laguna” seemed to lie in the charms of its cast, whose lives appeared both familiar (arguments over who to take to prom) and outlandish (many lived in sprawling homes). Although the show aired only from 2004 to 2006, its cultural impact was significant, preceding other genre-defining reality series like “Keeping Up With the Kardashians” and “The Real Housewives” franchises. It also inspired successful spinoffs like “The Hills” and “The City,” and it minted reality TV personalities like Lauren Conrad and Kristin Cavallari, whose celebrity endured long after they finished filming.Now, 20 years after the series finale aired on MTV, and as millennial nostalgia hits its peak, the cast has gathered for the first time to film a reunion-style special debuting on Roku. (Ms. Conrad, Ms. Cavallari and their co-star Stephen Colletti are executive producers.)The trailer dropped this week, and according to the streamer, the special will feature “behind-the-scenes stories, cast reactions to iconic episodes and a return to Laguna Beach” as the cast reflects “on the friendships, love triangles and drama that changed their lives.” It comes at a time when many millennial-era reboots are hitting screens, including a sequel to “The Devil Wears Prada” and a “Scrubs” revival.Here are the details on “The Reunion: Laguna Beach.”ImageCast members of “Laguna Beach: The Real Orange County,” which premiered in 2004. Credit...Jason Merritt/FilmMagicRelated ContentAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
